(FICTIONAL SAMPLE - prepared to demonstrate the case search system. Not a real case; no) Tj T*
(real persons.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(IN THE HIGH COURT OF EXAMPLE STATE \(FICTIONAL\)) Tj T*
(CRIMINAL APPEAL No. 2001 of 2026) Tj T*
(The appellant \(accused No. 1\) versus The State of Example State) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(SUMMARY OF THE PROSECUTION CASE) Tj T*
(The deceased, a woman aged about 45 years who sold vegetables in the weekly market, left) Tj T*
(her house on the evening of 3 March 2024 and did not return. Her son lodged a missing) Tj T*
(person complaint the next morning. On 9 March 2024 her body was recovered from a pit dug) Tj T*
(in a coconut grove, on the basis of a disclosure statement said to have been made by the) Tj T*
(appellant while in police custody, under Section 27 of the Indian Evidence Act. Gold) Tj T*
(ornaments said to belong to the deceased were recovered from the house of the) Tj T*
(appellant's brother-in-law.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is no eyewitness. The prosecution case rests entirely on circumstantial evidence:) Tj T*
(\(i\) the deceased was last seen in the company of the appellant near the bus stand at) Tj T*
(about 7 p.m. on 3 March 2024, according to a tea stall owner \(PW-2\); \(ii\) the recovery) Tj T*
(of the dead body at the instance of the appellant; \(iii\) the recovery of the ornaments;) Tj T*
(\(iv\) a spade with soil stains seized from the appellant's house; and \(v\) the appellant's) Tj T*
(alleged absconding for six days.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The trial court convicted the appellant under Sections 302 and 201 of the Indian Penal) Tj T*
(Code and sentenced him to imprisonment for life. The High Court is asked to set the) Tj T*
(conviction aside.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(GROUNDS OF APPEAL) Tj T*
(1. The last seen theory cannot by itself sustain a conviction, particularly when six) Tj T*
(days passed between the last sighting and the recovery of the body and other persons) Tj T*
(could have come in between.) Tj T*
(2. The disclosure statement was not proved as Section 27 of the Evidence Act requires:) Tj T*
(the exact words of the accused were not recorded, and the place of the recovery was open) Tj T*
(to the public and already known to the villagers.) Tj T*
(3. The recovery of the ornaments from the house of a third person is not linked to the) Tj T*
(appellant, and the seizure witnesses turned hostile.) Tj T*
(4. The chain of circumstances is incomplete and does not exclude every hypothesis) Tj T*
(consistent with the innocence of the accused, as the five golden principles in Sharad) Tj T*
(Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra require.) Tj T*
(5. No motive has been proved.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(RELIEF SOUGHT) Tj T*
(Set aside the conviction and sentence, and acquit the appellant of all charges.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
